These kernel modules have been tested on: HP Z230 SFF and HP Z2 machines with PCIe capabilities of Gen3x2 or higher.

Distros: Ubuntu 16.04 and 18.04
Kernel versions: 4.15 and 5.4+

Setup: SDX65LE EP attached over Oculink SFF-8612 cable to PCIe card with Oculink support.

Oculink PCIe adapter link:
https://www.amazon.com/PCIe-Lane-Oculink-SFF-8612-Adapter/dp/B07CVNNZM4/ref=sr_1_2?crid=22SDL2W16YCBE&keywords=oculink+pcie+sff-8612&qid=1561069494&s=electronics&sprefix=oculink+%2Celectronics%2C182&sr=1-2

--------------
Initial setup:
--------------

Power on SDX (should be spinning in PBL as PCIe early init DIP switch should be set)
Ensure host machine is powered off
Power on host (BIOS should detect SDX and a bootup of SDX should proceed as it receives PERST signal from host)
Once done, check if device has enumerated by running lspci (-nvv), you will see an entry for SDX65:
	01:00.0 Unassigned class [ff00]: Qualcomm Device 0308
Check if device config spaace access is available, run lspci -xx

Packages required:

Pre-requisites:
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install build-essential git

Optional:
sudo apt-get install screen

------
Setup:
------

Build and execute:

Run source build/envsetup.sh
Obtain kernel version information from the output

Run build/build.sh <mhi / pci / net / wwan / qrtr / all / clean> <all / clean>
	No arguments are needed to build all modules
Use build/install.sh to build and load all modules and have them auto-load at host bootup.
Use build/uninstall.sh to clean build and unload all modules and remove them from auto-load at host bootup list.
Use build/cross_compile.sh <valid kernel version> to build modules and place them in the "out" directory along with an install script.

Scripts:
Run scripts/load.sh <mhi / pci / net / wwan / qrtr / all> <-v> to load all KOs
	No arguments are needed to load all modules
	"-v" to enable dynamic debug for chosen or all modules (if using -v, please be root using sudo -s)
Run scripts/unload.sh <mhi / pci / net / wwan / qrtr / all> to unload all KOs
	No arguments are needed to unload all modules
Run scripts/status.sh <-v for verbose output / -u to monitor updates every second> to verify if modules are properly loaded and output states
Run scripts/debug.sh <-r to run logs> to enable dynamic debug (run as root or after sudo -s)
	Use | tee <filename> to redirect output to a file

----------------------
PCIe link information:
----------------------

Check PCIe gen/lane configurations:

cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/current_link_speed
cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/current_link_width

Maximum speeds/lane width supported:

cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/max_link_speed
cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/max_link_width

lspci -nvvv for verbose output
lspci -x for config space reads (all FF reads suggest link is down/inaccessible)

-----------------
Power management:
-----------------

Modify Runtime PM:

Disable: echo on > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/power/control
Enable: echo auto > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/power/control
Change timeout: echo > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/power/autosuspend_delay_ms

Testing System PM:
sudo rtcwake -m freeze -s 10 (Freeze and resume after 10 seconds)
sudo rtcwake -m disk -s 10 (Freeze and resume after 10 seconds)

-------------------------------
MHI devices/client information:
-------------------------------

Use debugfs for general information: /sys/kernel/debug/mhi/mhi0

Force EDL mode from host:
echo 1 > /sys/bus/mhi/devives/mhi0/force_edl

Trigger download of an image in EDL mode:
echo 1 > /sys/bus/mhi/devives/mhi0/edl_download

Force crash on device:
echo c > /proc/sysrq-trigger (on device via console/ADB)

Check serial number of SDX:
cat /sys/bus/mhi/devices/mhi0/serial_number

Available character devices can be found using:

ls /dev | grep mhi
ls /dev | grep wwan

-------------
Char devices:
-------------

EE: Mission mode:

mhi0_LOOPBACK
mhi0_QDSS
mhi0_IP_HW_ADPL
wwan0p1QCDM
wwan0p2MBIM
wwan0p3QMI
wwan0p4AT

EE: SBL:
mhi0_SAHARA

EE: FP (Flash Programmer):
wwan0p1FIREHOSE

-------------------
Network interfaces:
-------------------

root@pcie-sw-3-linux:/dev# ifconfig -a | grep mhi

mhi_hwip0: flags=144<POINTOPOINT,NOARP> mtu 16384
mhi_swip0: flags=144<POINTOPOINT,NOARP> mtu 16384

----------------
Client examples:
----------------

Loopback test over UCI:

cat /dev/mhi0_LOOPBACK (on host)
while true; do cat /dev/mhi_pipe_0 | xargs -I % echo % > /dev/mhi_pipe_0; done (on device)
while true; do echo hello > /dev/mhi0_LOOPBACK; sleep 1; done (from host)
Verify if host receives 'hello' back each second
CTRL+C on the device and host window (in that order) to stop the test

QMI: sudo apt-get install libqmi-utils
QMI command to retrieve device mode:

$ sudo qmicli -d /dev/mhi0_QMI --dms-get-model [/dev/mhi0_QMI]
	Device model retrieved: Model: 'FN980m'

SW IP path:

Setup IP addresses for MHI SW IP interfaces:
	On Host: ifconfig mhi_swip0 192.168.1.172 up
	On SDX: ifconfig mhi_swip0 192.168.1.173 up

	Set IP forwarding on host: ip route add 192.168.1.173 via 192.168.1.172
				or ip route add (device IP addr) via (host IP addr)

	Do a ping test to make sure connectivity is OK and request/replies are being sent back and forth:
	On host: ping 192.168.1.173
	On SDX: ping 192.168.1.172

ADB setup:
	After above basic NW plumbing, do the following on device:
		mount -o remount,rw /
		mv /sbin/adbd /sbin/adbd_old
		ps | grep adbd | grep -v grep
		  390 root      0:00 /sbin/adbd
		kill -9 390

		Wait 10 seconds...

		mv /sbin/adbd_old /sbin/adbd
		/sbin/adbd &

	On host:
		adb connect 192.168.1.173:5555

